@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Peter Naur argued that the real program lives in the programmer's head and the
files are lossy carriers of it.  As humans and agents share that theory, the
supporting artifacts matter more.  Today those artifacts are ad-hoc markdown
files (@file{PLAN.md}, @file{CLAUDE.md}, @file{SKILL.md}, @file{MEMORY.md}).
Valsi makes the artifacts themselves the core workspace:

@example
Artifact = Data + Grammar + View + Actions + Keymap
@end example

The grammar is descriptive, not normative: a set of recognizers over plain
markdown, like a linguist's grammar rather than a validator's schema.  A file
that only partially matches is not invalid; it resolves less detail and gets
the subset of views and commands its structure supports.

Valsi's three commitments are @emph{structure over transcripts},
@emph{control over delegation}, and @emph{files over formats}.  The harness is
extensible while running: define a grammar, view, and commands live, evaluate,
and use them --- no rebuild.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@section With Guix

The repository ships @file{valsi.scm}, a package definition and reproducible
dev shell.

@example
guix build -f valsi.scm                  # build + byte-compile
guix shell -D -f valsi.scm -- make check # dev shell + full test suite
@end example

The pinned tool set is @code{nss-certs emacs emacs-markdown-mode}; the Emacs
floor is 29.1 and CI runs on 30.2.

@section With MELPA

A recipe is provided under @file{recipes/valsi}.  Once available:

@example
M-x package-install RET valsi RET
@end example

@section From source

Clone the repository, then:

@example
make check           # byte-compile (warnings->errors) + ERT
make run             # launch a demo Emacs with Valsi loaded
@end example

Add @file{lisp/} to your @code{load-path} and @code{(require 'valsi)}.  Enable
@code{valsi-global-mode} to auto-activate on markdown buffers; the per-artifact
keymap is on @kbd{C-c n} with a @code{transient} menu on @kbd{C-c n m}.

@node Artifacts and grammars
@chapter Artifacts and grammars

Every artifact parses to a tree of typed nodes overlaid on the buffer; each
node keeps its region, so views and edits round-trip losslessly and a parse
never rewrites unrecognized text (the descriptive invariant, tested as
@code{parse@tie{}->@tie{}serialize} identity over the whole corpus).

Capability follows structure along a six-rung @dfn{degradation ladder}: each
command declares the rung it needs and is gated or falls back below it.
Disabling the minor mode leaves the buffer and file byte-identical.

Each grammar carries an @dfn{evidence tier}: @code{standardized} (a documented
open standard), @code{converging} (a pattern across many independent sources),
or @code{emergent} (a few repos or one vendor).  Most agent-facing files are
emergent and are never presented as standards.

@node Plan family
@chapter Plan family

The flagship grammar recognizes plan/tasks files across the Spec-Kit, Kiro,
Superpowers, GSD, and plain dialects: task lines and state, ids and sort keys,
tags, inline dependencies, trace links, file manifests, and verification
blocks.  It offers navigation, state and structure editing (toggle, insert,
split, promote/demote, atomic renumber with reference rewriting), a lint +
flymake backend (dangling deps, duplicate ids, cycles, contradictions), and
cross-artifact views: trace resolution, a requirements/tasks coverage table,
staleness against file mtime, a cross-file dashboard, and
@code{next-actionable} --- the agent-handoff primitive.  Wired to the agent
core it gains dispatch, a verification runner, and reviewable node-diffs.

@node Instruction family
@chapter Instruction family

The largest install base and weakest inherent structure --- the degradation
test.  It recognizes two scope axes: heading @emph{location} and frontmatter
@emph{glob predicate} (Cursor @code{globs:}, Copilot @code{applyTo:}), plus
instruction items, emphasis markers, @code{@@imports}, and @code{[[links]]}.
Commands: effective-instructions-at-point (nearest-wins), a transitive import
graph, lint, one-source-to-many @code{sync} via an idempotent managed region,
and scaffold.

@node Prompt-file family
@chapter Prompt-file family

The one place a near-mandatory schema lives at the top of the file.  One
grammar, three per-type vocabularies (skill, subagent, command) chosen by a
pure discriminator over the file's location and frontmatter.  The
progressive-disclosure load model is first-class: frontmatter is @emph{eager}
(always in context, the trigger), the body is @emph{lazy} (loaded on trigger).
Commands: validate (required keys, unknown-key warnings, the
description-as-trigger lint), complete a known key, @code{test-fire} (would
this prompt match?), and scaffold a skill directory.

@node Memory family
@chapter Memory family

The agent-written store: an @file{MEMORY.md} index of pointer lines over
per-fact record files, each with typed frontmatter
(@code{name}/@code{description}/@code{metadata.type} in @code{user},
@code{feedback}, @code{project}, @code{reference}) and @code{[[wiki-links]]}.
Beyond follow and backlinks it adds two curation commands that only report,
never merge: @code{dedupe} (duplicate pointer targets, near-identical record
descriptions) and @code{stale-check} (pointers whose file is gone, dangling
links).

@node Cross-artifact graph
@chapter Cross-artifact graph

The thesis capstone unifies the per-family links --- instruction
@code{@@imports}, memory @code{[[wiki]]} links and index pointers, and plan
path/trace refs plus phase-successor edges --- into one navigable edge list.
@kbd{RET} on a row visits the source artifact.  Edge sources are pluggable:
@code{valsi-graph-register-edge-source} lets a new family add edges with no
change to the core.

@node The agent core
@chapter The agent core

A standalone, artifact-agnostic brain: a provider abstraction with a
subscription-OAuth-first path (reusing Claude Code's token before any login)
and an API-key fallback, a typed tool registry with a scoping/confirmation
layer, a provider-neutral tool-use loop, and durable append-only JSONL
sessions.  It is client-side and rides MCP; it is deliberately @emph{not} part
of the AAP protocol surface.

@node The AAP protocol
@chapter The AAP protocol

Valsi is the reference implementation of the Agent Artifact Protocol, an
LSP-style JSON-RPC protocol for agent artifacts.  A server holds the model,
parser, and grammars as hot-registrable plugins; the Emacs client renders its
symbols, diagnostics, and edits.  AAP standardizes the envelope, node model,
and grammar-declaration --- never the specific grammars.  The specification is
@file{doc/aap-spec.md} (v0) and a machine-checkable conformance suite runs via
@code{make conformance}.

